1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,329
[Curator] Ladies and gentlemen,

2
00:00:02,329 --> 00:00:06,172
the court of history is back in session.

3
00:00:06,572 --> 00:00:09,689
[Curator] Today's case:
a Roman ruin in Wales,

4
00:00:09,689 --> 00:00:14,442
and a legend that calls it
King Arthur's Round Table.

5
00:00:15,942 --> 00:00:18,157
[Dig] Caerleon, in south Wales!

6
00:00:18,157 --> 00:00:23,442
A Roman fortress, home of
the Second Augustan Legion!

7
00:00:23,842 --> 00:00:25,722
[Dig] And outside the walls,

8
00:00:25,722 --> 00:00:27,602
a huge oval arena.

9
00:00:27,602 --> 00:00:31,662
Old maps label it King
Arthur's Round Table!

10
00:00:32,062 --> 00:00:33,897
[Clerk] Source: Museum Wales.

11
00:00:33,897 --> 00:00:39,702
The fortress was founded in A-D
seventy-four or seventy-five.

12
00:00:41,202 --> 00:00:44,313
[Curator] The Romans are not in doubt.

13
00:00:44,313 --> 00:00:51,054
Cadw says Caerleon was one of just three
permanent legionary fortresses in Britain,

14
00:00:51,054 --> 00:00:55,502
built to hold down a
fearsome Celtic tribe.

15
00:00:55,902 --> 00:01:01,392
[Curator] Museum Wales says it
held over five thousand soldiers.

16
00:01:01,792 --> 00:01:06,645
[Curator] The amphitheatre
was begun around A-D ninety,

17
00:01:06,645 --> 00:01:10,805
with room for about six thousand.

18
00:01:10,805 --> 00:01:14,572
That number is an estimate.

19
00:01:14,972 --> 00:01:16,676
[Curator] Cadw calls it

20
00:01:16,676 --> 00:01:20,952
the Roman equivalent of
today's multiplex cinema.

21
00:01:21,352 --> 00:01:22,309
[Doubt] A cinema.

22
00:01:22,309 --> 00:01:24,524
Not a throne room.

23
00:01:24,924 --> 00:01:29,934
[Clerk] Noted: one museum
page says A-D eighty.

24
00:01:31,434 --> 00:01:34,640
[Curator] The legend starts with a book:

25
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:38,380
The History of the Kings of Britain,

26
00:01:38,380 --> 00:01:42,954
written about eleven thirty-six
by Geoffrey of Monmouth.

27
00:01:43,354 --> 00:01:46,761
[Dig] He says Arthur
held a magnificent court

28
00:01:46,761 --> 00:01:49,194
in the City of Legions!

29
00:01:49,194 --> 00:01:50,954
That is Caerleon!

30
00:01:51,354 --> 00:01:54,052
[Clerk] Cadw describes Geoffrey as

31
00:01:54,052 --> 00:01:57,724
“the somewhat imaginative
twelfth-century scholar.”

32
00:01:58,124 --> 00:01:59,174
[Doubt] Somewhat.

33
00:02:00,674 --> 00:02:02,666
[Clerk] We searched Geoffrey's book:

34
00:02:02,666 --> 00:02:05,457
two translations and the Latin.

35
00:02:05,857 --> 00:02:08,687
[Clerk] No Round Table. No amphitheatre.

36
00:02:09,087 --> 00:02:11,747
[Curator] His guests sit at a table

37
00:02:11,747 --> 00:02:15,593
“according as the rank
of each did demand.”

38
00:02:15,993 --> 00:02:17,546
[Curator] The Round Table,

39
00:02:17,546 --> 00:02:19,617
where nobody ranks higher,

40
00:02:19,617 --> 00:02:25,093
first appears in a poem by
Wace, in eleven fifty-five.

41
00:02:25,493 --> 00:02:30,913
[Dig] So Arthur's table arrives
almost twenty years later!

42
00:02:31,313 --> 00:02:33,706
[Doubt] A table, I can believe.

43
00:02:33,706 --> 00:02:37,356
A table the size of a stadium?

44
00:02:38,856 --> 00:02:40,589
[Curator] In eleven eighty-eight,

45
00:02:40,589 --> 00:02:45,209
Gerald of Wales visited
the ruins at Caerleon.

46
00:02:45,209 --> 00:02:49,251
He listed palaces, baths,
temples and theatres.

47
00:02:49,251 --> 00:02:53,016
He never mentions a Round Table.

48
00:02:53,416 --> 00:02:55,558
[Dig] Then, in eighteen fifty-six,

49
00:02:55,558 --> 00:03:01,448
the poet Tennyson stays in
Caerleon, and writes in a letter:

50
00:03:01,448 --> 00:03:05,496
“I sit like King Arthur in Caerleon!”

51
00:03:05,896 --> 00:03:06,854
[Doubt] Lovely sentence.

52
00:03:06,854 --> 00:03:10,509
He does not mention the
amphitheatre either.

53
00:03:12,009 --> 00:03:16,907
[Curator] In nineteen twenty-six,
the amphitheatre was dug out

54
00:03:16,907 --> 00:03:20,269
amid a blaze of publicity.

55
00:03:20,669 --> 00:03:21,800
[Clerk] Source: Cadw.

56
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:25,760
The Daily Mail paid for the dig,

57
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:28,889
in return for exclusive updates.

58
00:03:29,289 --> 00:03:31,206
[Doubt] Cadw says the Wheelers

59
00:03:31,206 --> 00:03:35,819
“were canny enough not to
dispute this romantic idea.”

60
00:03:36,219 --> 00:03:41,002
[Curator] Cadw's own booklet
calls the Arthur story

61
00:03:41,002 --> 00:03:44,719
“an appealing, though untrue, story.”

62
00:03:45,119 --> 00:03:48,460
[Dig] But the digging was real!

63
00:03:48,460 --> 00:03:56,109
Cadw says Tessa Wheeler, Mortimer
Wheeler's wife, did the actual excavation!

64
00:03:56,509 --> 00:04:00,389
[Dig] And they found a lead curse tablet!

65
00:04:00,789 --> 00:04:01,710
[Clerk] Cadw's translation:

66
00:04:01,710 --> 00:04:07,233
“Lady Nemesis, I give thee
a cloak and a pair of boots.

67
00:04:07,233 --> 00:04:13,516
Let him who took them not redeem
them, unless with his own blood.“

68
00:04:13,916 --> 00:04:16,124
[Doubt] Now that is evidence.

69
00:04:17,624 --> 00:04:19,123
[Clerk] Roman amphitheatre: found.

70
00:04:19,123 --> 00:04:23,121
Round Table in Geoffrey: not in the text.

71
00:04:23,121 --> 00:04:25,620
Amphitheatre called the Round Table:

72
00:04:25,620 --> 00:04:28,618
the earliest firm label we found

73
00:04:28,618 --> 00:04:32,416
is on maps from around nineteen hundred.

74
00:04:32,816 --> 00:04:37,099
[Clerk] We could not
find when the name began.

75
00:04:38,599 --> 00:04:39,930
[Curator] The court finds:

76
00:04:39,930 --> 00:04:41,705
the Romans are real.

77
00:04:41,705 --> 00:04:43,479
The arena is real.

78
00:04:43,479 --> 00:04:46,441
The Round Table link is unproven.

79
00:04:47,641 --> 00:04:49,821
[Curator] Verdict: unproven.

80
00:04:50,221 --> 00:04:53,075
[Dig] But it is a great story!

81
00:04:53,475 --> 00:04:56,045
[Doubt] Great stories sell newspapers.

82
00:04:57,545 --> 00:05:02,708
[Curator] Next time: a pope puts a
dead pope on trial. Case adjourned.

